// stbdrive.bsm — nonlinear waveshaping (tube-style saturation) for b++ audio.
//
// Clean-room implementation. Tube saturation is soft-clipping — decades-old,
// textbook DSP — and the Fender circuit these functions evoke is public
// hardware. Nothing here derives from any third-party (GPL or other) source.
//
// A guitar amp's "drive" is a STATIC nonlinearity: a memoryless curve that
// rounds off tall peaks (soft clipping) and, on a real valve, rounds the two
// half-waves DIFFERENTLY (asymmetry → even harmonics → warmth). These are pure
// functions of one input sample — no state, so no struct and no alloc. The
// anti-aliasing that a hot nonlinearity needs (its harmonics fold back above
// Nyquist) is a separate concern handled by 2× oversampling / ADAA in a later
// increment; keep the shaping curve itself clean and testable here.
//
//   drive_soft   symmetric tanh soft-clip with input gain (odd harmonics only)
//   drive_tube   asymmetric valve-style drive (adds even harmonics, stays DC-free)

// Symmetric soft-clip: pre-gain the sample by `drive` (>= 1 pushes harder into
// the curve), then tanh it. tanh is an odd function, so a symmetric input makes
// only ODD harmonics — the fuzzy, aggressive edge of an overdriven sound.
// drive = 1 is the gentlest touch of saturation; drive = 10+ is heavy. The
// output is always bounded in (-1, 1) no matter how hard it is driven.
drive_soft(x: float, drive: float) -> float {
    return tanh_f(x * drive);
}

// Asymmetric valve drive. A real tube's transfer curve is not centered, so the
// positive and negative swings clip by different amounts — and that asymmetry is
// exactly what adds EVEN harmonics (the "warm", "tubey" body a symmetric clipper
// can never make). We model it by biasing the input before the soft-clip and
// then subtracting the DC that the bias itself produces, so the signal stays
// centered at silence (no thump, no offset drift):
//
//     y = ( tanh(drive*x + bias) - tanh(bias) ) * level
//
// `bias` sets the amount of asymmetry (0 = perfectly symmetric = drive_soft);
// `level` is the post-shaper output trim. At x = 0 the two tanh terms cancel,
// so silence in gives exact silence out.
drive_tube(x: float, drive: float, bias: float, level: float) -> float {
    return (tanh_f(x * drive + bias) - tanh_f(bias)) * level;
}
